; DOMAIN: mathematics/measure-theory/ergodic-decomposition [CLOSED, derived 2026-08-01]; Claim: Ergodic decomposition theorem states that any invariant measure for a measurable transformation can be decomposed into ergodic components. Given a measure-preserving system (X, B, mu, T), there exists a measurable partition of X into T-invariant subsets on which T is ergodic. Equivalently, mu can be written as an integral of ergodic measures over a probability space. Key concepts include ergodicity (no nontrivial invariant sets), Birkhoff's ergodic theorem (time averages equal space averages), Krylov-Bogolyubov theorem (existence of invariant measures), and entropy as an invariant. Applications span dynamical systems, statistical mechanics, number theory (equidistribution), and probability theory.; Result: The ergodic decomposition theorem is a cornerstone of ergodic theory, showing that any invariant measure decomposes into ergodic components — the "atoms" of measure-preserving dynamics. Birkhoff's ergodic theorem guarantees that time averages converge to space averages almost everywhere, justifying statistical mechanics assumptions. The decomposition allows analyzing complex systems by reducing to ergodic pieces. Metric entropy (Kolmogorov-Sinai) classifies systems up to measure-theoretic isomorphism. Mixing properties describe how systems forget initial conditions, with Bernoulli shifts being the strongest form of mixing.
; Bridge: genesis_measure_theory + genesis_statistical_mechanics + genesis_kinetic_theory + genesis_probability + genesis_topological_dynamics
(|- ((==> (&& (--> MeasurableSpace $X $B) (--> ProbabilityMeasure $mu) (--> MeasurableTransformation $T) (--> MeasurePreserving $T $mu))) (--> MeasurePreservingSystem $X $B $mu $T)) (stv 1.0 0.9))
;; Ergodicity means no nontrivial T-invariant measurable sets
(|- ((==> (&& (--> MeasurePreservingSystem $X $B $mu $T) (--> InvariantSet $A) (--> Or (--> (--> Measure $A 0) (--> Measure $A 1))))) (--> Ergodic $T $mu)) (stv 1.0 0.9))
(|- ((==> (&& (--> MeasurePreservingSystem $X $B $mu $T) (--> InvariantMeasure $mu) (--> ProbabilitySpace $Y) (--> ErgodicMeasure $nu))) (--> ErgodicDecomposition (Integral $mu $nu))) (stv 1.0 0.9))
;; Birkhoff ergodic theorem: time averages converge to space averages almost everywhere
(|- ((==> (&& (--> MeasurePreservingSystem $X $B $mu $T) (--> IntegrableFunction $f) (--> Ergodic $T $mu))) (--> BirkhoffTheorem (Converge (TimeAverage $f $T) (SpaceAverage $f $mu)))) (stv 1.0 0.9))
;; Krylov-Bogolyubov theorem: existence of invariant measures on compact spaces
(|- ((==> (&& (--> CompactSpace $X) (--> ContinuousMap $T) (--> BorelMeasure $mu))) (--> KrylovBogolyubov (InvariantMeasure $T $mu))) (stv 1.0 0.85))
;; Ergodic components are T-invariant subsets on which T is ergodic
(|- ((==> (&& (--> MeasurePreservingSystem $X $B $mu $T) (--> InvariantSubset $E) (--> Ergodic $T $mu))) (--> ErgodicComponent $E))) (stv 1.0 0.85))
;; Metric entropy (Kolmogorov-Sinai) is a measure-theoretic invariant of dynamical systems
(|- ((==> (&& (--> MeasurePreservingSystem $X $B $mu $T) (--> Partition $P) (--> Supremum $S))) (--> MetricEntropy (KS-Entropy $T $mu $P))) (stv 1.0 0.85))
;; Mixing: stronger property where correlations decay, implying ergodicity
(|- ((==> (&& (--> MeasurePreservingSystem $X $B $mu $T) (--> Forall $A $B) (--> Converge (Correlation $A $B $n) (Product $mu(A) $mu(B))))) (--> Mixing $T $mu)) (stv 1.0 0.85))
